Article 1 A Primary People's Court shall apply these Provisions when trying simple civil cases pursuant to Article 157 of the Civil Procedure Law, except for cases under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the defendant's whereabouts are unknown at the time of prosecution;
(2) the case is remanded for retrial;
(3) one party or both parties in a joint action are numerous in number;
(4) the law provides that special procedure, trial supervision procedure, summary procedure for urging repayment, public summons procedure, or bankruptcy and debt repayment procedure for enterprise legal persons shall apply; or
(5) the People's Court considers that it is inappropriate to apply summary procedure for adjudication.

Article 8 Where the People's Court is unable to notify the defendant to respond to the action pursuant to the service address or other contact information provided by the plaintiff, it shall handle the matter separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) where the plaintiff has provided the defendant's accurate service address, but the People's Court is unable to serve the notice of responding to the action directly upon the defendant or by substituted service, the case shall be transferred to the ordinary procedure for adjudication;
(2) where the plaintiff cannot provide the defendant's accurate service address, and the People's Court is still unable to ascertain the defendant's service address after examination, the court may rule to reject the plaintiff's prosecution on the ground that the defendant is unclear.

Article 14 For the following civil cases, the People's Court shall conduct mediation first when holding a hearing:
(1) disputes over marriage and family and succession;
(2) disputes over labor service contracts;
(3) damages disputes arising from traffic accidents and work-related injuries with relatively clear rights and obligations;
(4) disputes over homestead and neighboring relations;
(5) disputes over partnership contracts;
(6) disputes with a relatively small amount in dispute.
Except, however, where mediation cannot be conducted or is obviously unnecessary in light of the nature of the case and the actual circumstances of the parties.

Article 24 The court clerk shall record all activities of trying a civil case under the summary procedure in the record. The following matters shall be recorded in detail:
(1) major matters such as the judicial personnel's notification of the parties' litigation rights and obligations, summary of the disputed issues, determination of evidence, and pronouncement of judgment;
(2) major matters such as the parties' application for withdrawal, admission, withdrawal of the action, and settlement;
(3) other matters stated by the parties in court that are directly related to their litigation rights.

Article 32 For civil cases tried under the summary procedure, where any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court may appropriately simplify the part of the judgment document concerning the determination of facts or the reasons for the judgment when making the judgment document:
(1) the parties have reached a mediation agreement and a civil mediation document needs to be made;
(2) one party clearly states during the litigation that it admits all or part of the other party's litigation claims;
(3) the parties have no dispute or have minor disputes over the facts of the case;
(4) in a case involving a natural person's privacy, personal information, or trade secrets, one party requests simplification of the relevant contents in the judgment document, and the People's Court considers the reason to be just; or
(5) both parties unanimously agree to simplify the judgment document.
